      "content": "it's interesting that you call the other arguments \"not supported by empirical evidence.\" if they aren't based on evidence, then why do so many actual policy makers and international organizations take them seriously? it feels like you're saying the deterrence side is the only one based on \"reality\" while the other side is just wishful thinking. shouldn't we be looking at the actual risks of accidental launch or escalation as a valid, evidence-based counter to the deterrence theory?",
